der (by last_trial ascending).\n\nAnd \"Return complete retained entries and their signatures.\" The signatures array should just list the signatures of the retained entries in the same order as memory.\n\nLet's design the algorithm:\n\nInput case:\n- capacity: integer (maximum number of memory entries to retain)\n- trials: list of objects, each with signature (string), reflection (string), success (boolean). No trial index is given, but we can use the index in the list as the trial number (0-based or 1-based). The output's last_trial should be an integer. Which index? The description says \"last_trial\" - likely the trial number (0-indexed or 1-indexed?). Need to infer from examples? The fixture payload doesn't show expected output; we need to deduce from the description. Usually, trial number refers to the order index. I'll use 0-based index as the trial number.\n\nSteps:\n1. Initialize an empty list memory = [] (list of dicts with last_trial, reflection, signature, success).\n2. Iterate over trials with index i:\n   - Create entry: {last_trial: i, reflection: trial['reflection'], signature: trial['signature'], success: trial['success']}\n   - But before adding, check deduplication: if entry's success == False and there is already an entry with same signature and success == False, we should replace that entry (keeping the newer one). Actually \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" That means for failures, we keep only the most recent per signature. So we can check if there is an existing failure entry with same signature; if yes, remove it (or replace) and then add new one. But we also need to consider capacity? The deduplication should be applied before truncation? The description says \"When capacity binds, prioritize...\" So we first process all trials, building a memory that might exceed capacity, then truncate according to priority. But deduplication should happen during processing (or after) to avoid keeping multiple entries for same failure signature.\n\nBetter: For each trial, we add the entry, but if it's a failure, we remove any previous failure entry with same signature (keeping only the newest). For successes, we simply add (no deduplication). This matches \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature\". That means multiple successes with same signature can coexist? Possibly, but then when capacity binds, failures win.\n\nAlternative interpretation: \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature\" could mean that failures are deduplicated across the whole memory (so only one per failure signature). But successes are also deduplicated? Probably not, because it specifically says \"failure\". So we'll assume only failures are deduplicated.\n\nThen after processing all trials, we have a list of entries (some failures replaced). This list's size may exceed capacity. Then we need to truncate to capacity using priority: first all failures, then successes; within each group, sort by recency (descending last_trial? But \"then recency and signature\" suggests we sort entries within each group by recency (more recent first) and then by signature (lexicographic?) to break ties. Then keep the top 'capacity' entries according to the priority order: all failures (sorted by recency then signature) first, then successes (sorted similarly). But after truncation, we need to restore chronological order. So we take the selected entries and sort them by last_trial ascending to get final memory.\n\nEdge case: if we remove failures during deduplication, the last_trial for that signature is the newest.\n\nAlso, note: \"signatures\" output is just an array of signatures from the retained memory entries in same order as memory.\n\nImplementation details: Use standard library only. No randomness.\n\nLet's test with the provided cases mentally? We don't have expected output, but we can reason.\n\nCase 000: capacity=3, trials:\n0: sig e0, success true\n1: sig e3, success true\n2: sig e2, success true\n3: sig e1, success false\n4: sig e1, success true\n5: sig e1, success false\n6: sig e1, success false\n\nProcess:\n- i0: add success e0\n- i1: add success e3\n- i2: add success e2\n- i3: failure e1 -> add. memory now 4 entries.\n- i4: success e1 -> add. 5 entries.\n- i5: failure e1 -> since failure with same sig exists (i3), remove i3, add i5. Now memory: e0, e3, e2, e1(success i4), e1(failure i5). That's 5 entries.\n- i6: failure e1 -> remove previous failure i5, add i6. Memory: e0, e3, e2, e1(success i4), e1(failure i6). 5 entries.\n\nNow capacity=3. Prioritize failures first. Here only one failure: e1 (i6). Then successes: e0,i0; e3,i1; e2,i2; e1(success,i4). Sort successes by recency: i4 (latest) then i2, i1, i0? Actually recency descending: i4 (last_trial 4), i2, i1, i0. Then by signature? But recency is primary. So successes sorted: i4, i2, i1, i0. Then we take top capacity=3: first the failure (1 entry), then the most recent successes: take first 2 successes from sorted list (i4 and i2). That gives entries: failure e1 (i6), success e1 (i4), success e2 (i2). Then restore chronological order: sort by last_trial: i2 (2), i4 (4), i6 (6). So memory: [e2 success, e1 success, e1 failure]? Wait order: i2 is e2 success, i4 is e1 success, i6 is e1 failure. That yields signatures: ['e2','e1','e1']? But there are two e1 signatures. That seems okay. However, note that we have two entries with same signature e1 (one success, one failure). That's allowed because deduplication only applies to failures. The output signatures array should be ['e2','e1','e1']? Actually the order of memory is chronological: last_trial 2,4,6. So signatures: 'e2','e1','e1'. That matches.\n\nBut wait, we also need to ensure that we don't keep both failure and success for same signature? The dedup only removes duplicate failures. So it's fine.\n\nBut we also need to consider that success e1 (i4) and failure e1 (i6) are different entries. That is plausible.\n\nNow check if the algorithm aligns with the description: \"When capacity binds, prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature.\" In our truncation, we sorted failures first by recency, but we only had one failure. For successes, we sorted by recency then signature. The description says \"then recency and signature\" meaning after separating failures and successes, within each group sort by recency then signature. We did that. Good.\n\nNow, what about the case where there are multiple failures? We sort failures by recency descending, then by signature. Then we take as many failures as possible until capacity, then fill with successes.\n\nBut note: \"prioritize failures before successes\" could mean we keep all failures first, then if we have remaining capacity, we keep successes. So if failures exceed capacity, we keep the most recency/signature-sorted failures up to capacity, and no successes. That makes sense.\n\nAfter truncation, we restore chronological order. That is final step.\n\nNow implement solve(case). Use Python.\n\nNote: The output must be a JSON-serializable object. Use json module if needed but not required. Return dict.\n\nAlso, the function must not mutate case. We'll copy data.\n\nLet's write code.\n\nPotential edge: The payload may have extra fields like \"_case_nonce\". We ignore.\n\nImplementation in solve:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    capacity = case['capacity']\n    trials = case['trials']\n    memory = []  # list of dicts\n    # keep track of failure entries by signature for replacement:\n    # We'll store index in memory for each failure signature? But we need to remove and add.\n    # Simpler: maintain a dict for failures: signature -> (index in memory). When we add a new failure, if exists, pop that entry, then add new one. We'll need to update indices after removal? Since we only remove one entry at a time, we could just rebuild? But efficiency not critical as trials small. We'll do straightforward: when processing a failure, check if any existing failure with same signature in current memory; if yes, replace it (remove that entry and add new). We can do this by filtering memory and rebuilding? Simpler: keep separate list for failures? But memory includes successes too. We'll just scan memory each time to find failure with same sig. Acceptable.\n\nBut careful: when we replace a failure, we must remove the old entry and add the new one. The order in memory should reflect trial order? But dedup says we keep newest reflection, so we replace with new entry. That new entry's last_trial is current index. So we can just remove the old failure entry and append the new one at the end (since we are processing in order, new is later). That preserves chronological order? Not exactly: if we remove an earlier entry and append at end, the chronological order of the memory list no longer matches trial order (since the new entry is at end but its trial index is later). However, after all processing, we will have to reorder by last_trial anyway, because \"Restore chronological order in the final memory.\" So it's fine to have memory in arbitrary order during processing, as long as we sort at the end. So we can treat memory as a set of entries, and when adding, we just add to list. For dedup, we remove the previous failure entry (whatever its position) and add the new one at the end. So the final list before truncation will have entries in an order that is not chronological. Then we sort after truncation.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Initialize memory = []\n- For each trial index i, trial in enumerate(trials):\n    entry = {\"last_trial\": i, \"reflection\": trial['reflection'], \"signature\": trial['signature'], \"success\": trial['success']}\n    if not entry['success']:\n        # dedup: remove any existing failure with same signature\n        # use list comprehension to remove? We can find indices and pop.\n        # Since we are modifying list while iterating, we can do after adding? Better: first remove, then add.\n        # We'll find the first (o